Original commit body from 8ed2b44bd1987cd515aa148cff0876c7511a6668:

The standard/wide table is now an ItemTable (a DataTable subclass) that
reports row clicks. A Shift+click posts RowShiftClicked and is consumed:
prevent_default stops DataTable's own click handler, which Textual would
otherwise run next in the MRO walk as a plain click. Other row clicks post
RowPlainClicked and proceed as usual.

- Shift+click toggles the clicked row in the selection; starting a
  selection also takes the row highlighted before the click, so
  highlight-then-Shift+click selects both. The cursor follows the click.
- Space toggles the highlighted row: the keyboard route, for terminals
  that keep Shift+click for their own text selection and never report it.
- Selected rows carry a check in the # column (drawn by the populate and
  chain-highlight passes, which rewrite that column on every highlight).
- The right panel shows "(N items selected)", with a note that other keys
  act on the highlighted row.
- Mark Read / Mark Unread apply to every selected item through one
  _set_human_read(): one preferences save, one in-place rebuild. The
  selection stays, so Mark Unread undoes it in one click.
- Esc or a plain click clears the selection. A rebuild drops selected
  items that are no longer listed (a read unread row that a filter hides),
  so a batch never reaches an item the user cannot see. Tree view and
  compact tier have no multi-select and drop it.

Tests: TestMultiSelect drives real Shift+clicks through the pilot (the
tui_pilot.click helper gains shift=). Tracker package coverage 100% in
isolation.

